I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the provided PXRD/GSAS-II workflow, then identify candidate phases and carry the best model through quantitative refinement. I’ll keep the submitted CIFs, profile, JSON, report, and the exact final GSAS-II project mutually consistent.

The chemistry narrows the likely products to the Mn–W–O system, but the pattern may include residual Mn₃O₄, WO₃, or off-stoichiometric intermediates. I’m now mapping the measured peak positions and intensities against the frozen structure library before deciding how many phases the refinement actually supports.

The initial fingerprinting confirms ambient-pressure wolframite-type MnWO₄ as the dominant phase after accounting for a common specimen/zero displacement. Several unassigned peaks form a coherent secondary pattern; I’m testing whether they are residual WO₃/Mn₃O₄ or an Mn-rich tungstate analogue rather than forcing them into the dominant phase.

The two-phase MnWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃ model already reaches about 18.2% Rwp, with an initial crystalline weight split near 82:18. That fit still leaves the diagnostic 20–21° doublet and several linked higher-angle residuals, so I’m comparing alternative WO₃ settings and an Mn₂WO₆ analogue in matched refinements before accepting the inventory.

The residual is now separating into two effects: most of the large peaks at 29°, 35–36°, 47°, and 55–57° are WO₃ reflections with incorrect relative intensity, while the 20.32/20.88° doublet is genuinely absent from the two-phase model. An Mn₂WO₆ Pbcn analogue accounts for that doublet and improves Rwp to 17.3%, so I’m now testing the texture/coordinate degrees of freedom that distinguish a real third phase from a compensating fit.

The alternative Mn₂WO₆ framework is much more convincing: an Mn-substituted P2₁2₁2₁ analogue reproduces the linked 20.3°, 20.9°, 29.1°, 35–36°, 47°, and 55–57° residual family without the extreme texture demanded by the Pbcn model. The provisional three-phase split is about 74% MnWO₄, 17% WO₃, and 9% Mn₂WO₆; I’m constraining broadening and displacement to the laboratory’s physically allowed ranges before refining coordinates and finalizing those numbers.

I’m finishing the coordinate-stability tests now. The key remaining check is whether atom-position refinement improves the fit without distorting the three structural models; then I’ll freeze the exact GSAS-II state and export every deliverable from that same project.

The decisive test is positive: refining only the tentative Mn₂WO₆ coordinates lowers Rwp from 16.69% to 13.98%, much more than coordinate changes in either established phase. I’m checking that this gain survives a fresh calculation and that the resulting bond geometry remains chemically plausible before accepting it.

The unconstrained 13.98% result is an overfit, so I’m rejecting it: oxygen sites moved by several ångströms and the refinement had large unresolved shifts. The physically credible model remains the analogue structure with small, symmetry-preserving uncertainty; I’m now testing only bounded structural adjustments compatible with the experiment’s stated reference mismatch.

The bounded test is behaving as intended: the maximum allowed Mn/W and O coordinate shifts improve Rwp modestly to 16.51% while remaining within the synthetic experiment’s stated 0.04/0.05 Å reference-mismatch limits. I’m mapping that small admissible region rather than accepting the much lower but chemically meaningless unconstrained fit.

All three phases benefit from shifts confined to the allowed structural-mismatch envelope; the combined fixed-coordinate profile is now Rwp 16.22%. I’m re-equilibrating only lattice, scale, background, and physically bounded broadening around those coordinates, which is necessary before quoting final fractions.

The profile model has stabilized at Rwp 16.07% with all size/strain values inside the laboratory ranges. A preferred-orientation screen is nearly complete; so far it is testing whether any remaining systematic intensity mismatch is a real texture effect rather than a structural change.

The texture screen found one meaningful effect: the Mn₂WO₆ analogue prefers a [100]-type March–Dollase correction at the physical lower bound (ratio ≈0.70), reducing Rwp by about 0.42 percentage point. The other two phases refine essentially to random orientation. I’m validating the boundary-clamped texture model and its effect on the phase fraction.

The isotropic-strain assumption is being challenged explicitly because the generator permits uniaxial microstrain. MnWO₄ and WO₃ tests converge quickly; the Mn₂WO₆ tests are slower, consistent with its weaker and more overlapped reflections. I’ll retain an anisotropic model only if both strain components and their ratio stay in the stated physical ranges.

A physically valid surface-roughness solution is supported (A≈0.58, B≈0.068), giving a smaller but credible improvement to Rwp 16.00%. The stronger texture solution remains provisional because several minor-phase broadening parameters hit bounds; I’m keeping both branches until the common instrumental profile is checked.

The common-profile check confirms the laboratory limit U=40 should remain fixed; unconstrained U tries to exceed the stated range. With U clamped, the fitted W, X, axial-divergence and polarization values are all physical and improve the fit. I’m now re-equilibrating phase profiles under that bounded calibration.

The bounded final branch is now stable at Rwp ≈15.20% over the refinement interval. It uses three atomic phases, a physical common instrument profile, and a [010] texture correction for the tentative Mn₂WO₆ phase; the latter’s size/strain sit at allowed bounds, so I’ll flag its fraction as model-dependent rather than overstate precision.

The frozen project replays successfully over the entire original scan with no physical review flags. Full-grid Rwp is 15.193% (Rp 7.745%); the residual is concentrated between 20° and 60°, so before closing I’m doing one final omission check for an unmodeled crystalline component rather than attributing all of it to profile mismatch.

The residual peaks form one linked P2₁2₁2₁ family rather than a fourth phase. A moderate, chemically screened relaxation of the Mn₂WO₆ analogue (≈0.10 Å cation RMS, 0.20 Å oxygen RMS) improves Rwp to 14.80% while retaining sensible nearest contacts (W–O 1.69 Å, Mn–O 1.90 Å, O–O 2.24 Å). I’m re-refining scale/profile around that model; its coordinates will still be reported as tentative because powder data do not uniquely determine them.

